A send that fails must not be silent, and a reply must be traceable at both ends.

MONEYMONEY NEVER LEFT ITS SPLASH WITH LAUNCHD RUNNING, and every layer between the two processes
reported success. handle_send_result is where the truth was: every caller ignores its return value
and its only report is an xpc_log_debug with no sink in this container, so a send that failed and a
send that arrived looked identical from outside the process.

What the trace then said, and it named a bug two layers down:

    CIDER_XPC send FAILED reason=4 err=0x10000002 id=0x10000000 dest=0xb03

reason 4 is DISPATCH_MACH_MESSAGE_SEND_FAILED and 0x10000002 is MACH_SEND_INVALID_DATA, which
ipc_kmsg_get returns when copyinmsg cannot read the message out of the sending process.

THE SENDER'S OWN IDENTITY IS IN THE LINE (pid and pthread) because that is what tied it to the
daemon-side failure: the pid that could not be read was the sender's, and procfs called it a zombie.

DEFERRED IS NOT DELIVERED and is named separately: DISPATCH_MACH_NEEDS_DEFERRED_SEND leaves the
message queued on the channel, and a daemon that exits right after replying takes the queue with it.

WHICH PATH SENT IT, because a synchronous send reports only after its wait ends and an asynchronous
one reports immediately, so the same line means opposite things: sync-returned says the caller
already has its answer, async says it is still waiting. That distinction is what showed the trust
round trip completing and a SECOND request going unanswered.

AND WHERE A REPLY LANDS: dmxh_async_reply_handler, which the channel handler's trace never covers, so
its silence used to prove nothing.

All of it gated on a NON-EMPTY CIDER_TRACE_XPC, unlike the older traces in this file: drivers here
export every switch empty, and getenv != NULL is true for those.
